KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11 --unwind 12 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ no read error: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ 0 stored on failure: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The num_get facet is not modelled.  With libstdc++, num_get::get reads a
nondeterministic facet pointer (dereference failures) and __check_facet
calls std::__throw_bad_cast, which has no body; with libc++,
locale::use_facet has no body and get() dereferences a nondeterministic
__shared_count.  On the remaining paths cin.fail() always holds (assertion
3 holds) and i is not 0 (assertion 2 fails).  The facet of the "C" locale
is needed, as for num_put.
